@** The scene. Everything the viewer knows about a USD file passes through
one object of class [[Scene]]. The USD stage is itself the scene graph: the
user interface asks it directly for the hierarchy, the names and the types of
the prims. What the stage does not offer is the form of the data that a ray
tracer wants, and producing that form is the scene's work. It has three jobs.
First, it turns every visible piece of geometry into a compact indexed
triangle mesh, kept in the prim's own coordinate system and placed in the world
by a transform. Second, it resolves the materials of the scene: it follows
UsdShade connections to the inputs of the UsdPreviewSurface shader, and loads
the textures they name. Third, it collects the cameras of the stage, so that
the viewer can look through them.

The declarations live in \lpfile{Scene.h} and the code in \lpfile{Scene.cpp}.
The header is short enough to be read as a table of contents for this part.

