<<Headers needed by the scene declarations>>=
// A USD stage and what the renderer needs from it. The stage itself is the
// scene graph (hierarchy, names, types); the scene adds one indexed
// triangle mesh per renderable prim, its materials and textures, and the
// stage's cameras.
#pragma once

#include <array>
#include <map>
#include <string>
#include <tuple>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <vector>

#include <pxr/usd/usd/stage.h>
#include <pxr/usd/usdShade/material.h>
@* Meshes for the renderer. The renderer traces rays through a two-level
acceleration structure. At the bottom, every mesh has its own \emph{geometry
acceleration structure} (GAS), built over its triangles in the mesh's own
coordinates. At the top, one \emph{instance acceleration structure} (IAS)
holds an \emph{instance} per mesh: a reference to its GAS, a $3\times4$
transform that places it in the world, and a 32-bit instance id. This is the
shape in which OptiX expects a scene of many objects, and it fits USD well:
every prim has a local-to-world transform, so its points can stay exactly as
the file authors them, and the transform rides along in the instance. A moved
object changes only its instance; its GAS, the expensive part, stays valid.
The instance id is the index of the mesh, which the device programs use to
find its attributes and which the renderer writes into its picking buffer, so
that a click on the image leads back to the prim.

A [[RenderMesh]] is the record of one such mesh. Each vertex has a position
and, when the prim has them, a shading normal and texture coordinates, all in
the prim's local space. The triangles are given by [[indices]] and, in
parallel, by the material of each triangle in [[triMaterial]]: when a ray hits
triangle $t$ of a mesh, OptiX reports the index $t$, and that is all the
device needs to find the three vertices and the material. An empty
[[normals]] array means ``shade flat''; an empty [[uvs]] array means that the
prim has no texture coordinates. The remaining fields serve the inspector and
the camera: the path of the prim, the material bound to the prim itself, the
number of GeomSubsets that override it, how many triangle corners the prim had
before identical ones were merged, and the bounds of the mesh in world space.

<<A renderable mesh>>=
// One renderable prim (Mesh, Cube or Sphere) as an indexed triangle mesh in
// the prim's local space, placed in the world by `toWorld`. Vertices are the
// distinct corners of the source faces (position, shading normal and st),
// welded and reordered with meshoptimizer.
struct RenderMesh
{
    pxr::SdfPath path;
    std::array<float, 12> toWorld;          // row-major 3x4, acting on column vectors
    std::vector<float>        positions;    // xyz per vertex
    std::vector<float>        normals;      // xyz per vertex; empty for flat shading
    std::vector<float>        uvs;          // st per vertex; empty without texture coordinates
    std::vector<unsigned int> indices;      // 3 per triangle, counter-clockwise from outside
    std::vector<unsigned int> triMaterial;  // material of each triangle
    unsigned int material;         // material bound to the prim itself
    unsigned int subsetMaterials;  // number of GeomSubsets with their own material
    size_t corners;                // triangle corners before welding
    float boundsMin[3], boundsMax[3];  // world space

    size_t triangleCount() const { return indices.size() / 3; }
    size_t vertexCount() const { return positions.size() / 3; }
};
@ The [[Scene]] class is small from the outside. A scene is either empty or
holds one open stage; [[load]] replaces whatever was there. The accessors give
the user interface the stage, the meshes, materials, textures and cameras, and
the list of warnings collected while reading the file. The number of prims and
the total number of triangles are for the status bar. The up axis matters to
the camera, which must know whether ``up'' is $+y$ or $+z$, and the bounds of
the whole scene let it frame everything.

