@* Opening a stage. Loading starts from a clean slate. [[UsdStage::Open]]
composes the file with everything it references; if that fails, the error
message is returned to the caller, who shows it in a dialog.

Two global properties of the stage are recorded. The up axis is part of the
stage metadata and defaults to $y$. The time is the start of the authored
time range if there is one, and USD's \emph{default} time otherwise: the
viewer shows a single moment, and for an animated file the first frame is the
natural choice. Then we count the prims, extract the geometry and collect the
cameras.

The prims are walked with a [[UsdPrimRange]], which visits the stage depth
first, in the order of the file, skipping the invisible pseudo-root. The
predicate [[UsdTraverseInstanceProxies]] deserves a word. USD shares the
contents of \emph{instanceable} prims: all instances of a model point to a
single hidden \emph{prototype}, and by default the children of an instance are
not visited at all. With this predicate, the walk descends into every instance
and yields \emph{instance proxies}, read-only stand-ins for the prototype's
prims at the instance's location. Each instance therefore gets its own row in
the hierarchy panel and its own mesh, with its own copy of the triangles. The
two-level acceleration structure could let all instances of a prototype share
one GAS; the viewer keeps one mesh per prim, which is simpler, and costs
memory only in scenes with very many instances. The hierarchy panel walks the
stage with the same predicate, so the prim count and the tree agree.

<<Open a stage and build the scene>>=
bool Scene::load(const std::string& path, std::string& error)
{
    clear();
    UsdStageRefPtr stage = UsdStage::Open(path);
    if (!stage)
    {
        error = "Could not open USD stage: " + path;
        return false;
    }
    m_stage = stage;
    m_filePath = path;
    m_zUp = UsdGeomGetStageUpAxis(stage) == UsdGeomTokens->z;
    m_time = stage->HasAuthoredTimeCodeRange() ? UsdTimeCode(stage->GetStartTimeCode()) : UsdTimeCode::Default();
    const UsdPrimRange prims = UsdPrimRange::Stage(stage, UsdTraverseInstanceProxies());
    m_primCount = size_t(std::distance(prims.begin(), prims.end()));
    extractGeometry();
    extractCameras();
    return true;
}
@ Clearing forgets the meshes, materials, textures and cameras, empties the
index and both caches, and resets the bounds to the zero box. Releasing the
stage pointer closes the stage, unless somebody else still holds it.

<<Forget the current stage>>=
void Scene::clear()
{
    m_meshes.clear();
    m_meshByPath.clear();
    m_materials.clear();
    m_textures.clear();
    m_cameras.clear();
    m_warnings.clear();
    m_materialCache.clear();
    m_textureCache.clear();
    m_primCount = 0;
    std::fill_n(m_boundsMin, 3, 0.0f);
    std::fill_n(m_boundsMax, 3, 0.0f);
    m_stage = nullptr;
    m_filePath.clear();
}
@ Problems found while reading a file are not errors: an unsupported shader
or a missing texture should not prevent us from showing the rest of the scene.
They are collected as warnings, which the inspector lists. A large scene can
produce the same complaint for hundreds of prims, so each distinct message is
kept only once.

<<Collect a warning once>>=
void Scene::warn(const std::string& message)
{
    if (std::find(m_warnings.begin(), m_warnings.end(), message) == m_warnings.end())
        m_warnings.push_back(message);
}
@ The total number of triangles is summed over the meshes when asked; the
status bar asks once a frame, and a scene has far fewer meshes than triangles.

<<Count the triangles of the scene>>=
size_t Scene::triangleCount() const
{
    size_t count = 0;
    for (const RenderMesh& mesh : m_meshes)
        count += mesh.triangleCount();
    return count;
}
@ Meshes are looked up in a hash map filled as they are created, because the
user interface asks about the selected prim every frame and a large scene has
tens of thousands of meshes. Cameras are few, so a linear search by path is
enough.

<<Find the mesh or the camera of a prim>>=
int Scene::meshIndex(const SdfPath& path) const
{
    const auto it = m_meshByPath.find(path);
    return it == m_meshByPath.end() ? -1 : int(it->second);
}

int Scene::cameraIndex(const SdfPath& path) const
{
    for (size_t i = 0; i < m_cameras.size(); ++i)
        if (m_cameras[i].path == path.GetString())
            return int(i);
    return -1;
}
@* Extracting the geometry. Extraction is one pass over the prims. For each
prim that should be drawn we produce its triangles in local space, decide the
material of each face, read its texture coordinates, gather the attributes of
every triangle corner, weld the corners into an indexed mesh, and record the
transform that places it in the world. Each of these steps gets a section of
its own.

<<Extract the renderable meshes of the stage>>=
// ---- geometry ---------------------------------------------------------------
void Scene::extractGeometry()
{
    <<Prepare the extraction of the geometry>>

    for (const UsdPrim& p : UsdPrimRange::Stage(m_stage, UsdTraverseInstanceProxies()))
    {
        <<Skip prims that are not visible renderable geometry>>

        <<Triangulate a prim in its local space>>

        <<Assign a material to every face of a prim>>

        <<Read the texture coordinates of a prim>>

        <<Gather the corners of a prim's triangles>>

        <<Build the indexed mesh of a prim>>

        <<Pass the transform of a mesh to the renderer>>
        <<Compute the world bounds of a mesh>>

        <<Warn about textured prims without texture coordinates>>

        <<Register the mesh of a prim>>
    }

    <<Record the bounds of the scene>>
}
@ A [[UsdGeomXformCache]] computes local-to-world matrices and remembers the
matrices of ancestors, so that siblings deep in the tree do not recompute the
same chain of transforms. The bounds of the scene start empty, at $+\infty$
and $-\infty$.

<<Prepare the extraction of the geometry>>=
UsdGeomXformCache xformCache(m_time);
float lo[3] = {INFINITY, INFINITY, INFINITY}, hi[3] = {-INFINITY, -INFINITY, -INFINITY};
@ Only \emph{gprims}, the geometric primitives of UsdGeom, can be drawn.
Visibility is inherited, so we ask USD to \emph{compute} it rather than read
the prim's own attribute: a mesh under an invisible transform is invisible.
The \emph{purpose} works the same way; we draw what a final render would draw
(purpose [[default]] or [[render]]) and skip the [[proxy]] and [[guide]]
geometry that applications use for previews and manipulators.

<<Skip prims that are not visible renderable geometry>>=
if (!p.IsA<UsdGeomGprim>())
    continue;
const UsdGeomImageable imageable(p);
const TfToken purpose = imageable.ComputePurpose();
if (imageable.ComputeVisibility(m_time) == UsdGeomTokens->invisible ||
    (purpose != UsdGeomTokens->default_ && purpose != UsdGeomTokens->render))
    continue;
@* From prims to triangles. Three kinds of gprims are supported: polygonal
meshes, and the implicit cube and sphere. Each is converted into a
[[LocalMesh]], a triangle soup in the prim's own coordinate system. The other
gprims (cylinders, cones, capsules, curves, points) produce no triangles and
are skipped. The default sizes are USD's: a cube of edge~2 and a sphere of
radius~1.

<<Triangulate a prim in its local space>>=
LocalMesh local;
if (p.IsA<UsdGeomMesh>())
    meshFromUsdMesh(UsdGeomMesh(p), m_time, local);
else if (p.IsA<UsdGeomCube>())
{
    double size = 2.0;
    UsdGeomCube(p).GetSizeAttr().Get(&size, m_time);
    meshFromCube(size, local);
}
else if (p.IsA<UsdGeomSphere>())
{
    double radius = 1.0;
    UsdGeomSphere(p).GetRadiusAttr().Get(&radius, m_time);
    meshFromSphere(radius, local);
}
if (local.indices.empty())
    continue;
@ A triangle soup must remember more than three point indices per triangle.
USD attaches data to meshes at different rates, called \emph{interpolations}:
once per point ([[vertex]] or [[varying]]), once per face ([[uniform]]), once
per corner of each face ([[faceVarying]]), or once for the whole mesh
([[constant]]). To fetch the right value for each corner of each triangle
later, we keep, besides the point index, the \emph{face-vertex} index of the
corner (its position in USD's [[faceVertexIndices]] array) and the index of
the face the triangle came from. The face index also serves GeomSubsets, which
assign materials to sets of faces. The shading normals, when there are any,
are resolved per corner right away.

<<Local triangle soup of one prim>>=
// Local-space triangle soup produced for one prim.
struct LocalMesh
{
    std::vector<GfVec3f> points;
    std::vector<unsigned int> indices;     // 3 point indices per triangle
    std::vector<unsigned int> cornerFv;    // 3 face-vertex indices per triangle
    std::vector<unsigned int> faceOfTri;   // source face of each triangle
    std::vector<GfVec3f> cornerNormals;    // 3 shading normals per triangle, empty = flat
    size_t faceCount = 1;
};
@ A [[UsdGeomMesh]] describes polygons by three arrays: the points, the
number of vertices of each face, and the concatenated vertex indices of all
faces. Converting it takes four steps.

<<Convert a UsdGeomMesh into a local triangle soup>>=
bool meshFromUsdMesh(const UsdGeomMesh& mesh, UsdTimeCode time, LocalMesh& out)
{
