@ A mesh lacking any of the three arrays has nothing to draw.

<<Read the points and the face topology of a mesh>>=
VtArray<GfVec3f> points;
VtArray<int> counts, faceIndices;
if (!mesh.GetPointsAttr().Get(&points, time) || !mesh.GetFaceVertexCountsAttr().Get(&counts, time) ||
    !mesh.GetFaceVertexIndicesAttr().Get(&faceIndices, time))
    return false;
@ A face with $n$ vertices $v_0,\ldots,v_{n-1}$ becomes the $n-2$ triangles
$(v_0, v_k, v_{k+1})$ for $1\le k\le n-2$, a \emph{fan} around the first
vertex. This is exact for triangles and for convex polygons, which is what
real assets contain almost exclusively; a concave polygon may come out wrong,
and we accept that. The variable [[offset]] is the face-vertex index of the
face's first corner, so [[c0]], [[c1]] and [[c2]] are the face-vertex indices of
the triangle's corners.

Files are not always well formed, and a bad index would make the GPU read
outside its buffers. A negative or excessive vertex count ends the conversion;
a triangle that names a nonexistent point is dropped.

<<Fan-triangulate the faces of a mesh>>=
out.points.assign(points.begin(), points.end());
out.faceCount = counts.size();
size_t offset = 0;
for (size_t face = 0; face < counts.size(); ++face)
{
    const int count = counts[face];
    if (count < 0 || offset + count > faceIndices.size())
        break;
    for (int k = 1; k + 1 < count; ++k)  // fan triangulation
    {
        const size_t c0 = offset, c1 = offset + k, c2 = offset + k + 1;
        const int i0 = faceIndices[c0], i1 = faceIndices[c1], i2 = faceIndices[c2];
        if (i0 < 0 || i1 < 0 || i2 < 0 || size_t(std::max({i0, i1, i2})) >= points.size())
            continue;
        out.indices.insert(out.indices.end(), {unsigned(i0), unsigned(i1), unsigned(i2)});
        out.cornerFv.insert(out.cornerFv.end(), {unsigned(c0), unsigned(c1), unsigned(c2)});
        out.faceOfTri.push_back(unsigned(face));
    }
    offset += count;
}
@ USD meshes are right-handed by default: seen from the outside, the
vertices of a face go counter-clockwise, and the cross product of two edges,
$(p_1-p_0)\times(p_2-p_0)$, points outward. A mesh may declare itself
[[leftHanded]], with the opposite winding. The renderer relies on the
cross product to know which side of a triangle faces out (it needs this for
the normal-map frame and to orient shading normals), so we normalize the
winding here, once: swapping two corners of each triangle reverses it. The
face-vertex indices are swapped along, so that each corner keeps its own
texture coordinates and normals.

<<Rewind the triangles of a left-handed mesh>>=
// Make every triangle counter-clockwise when seen from outside, so the
// renderer can derive the outward normal (and the normal-map frame) from it.
TfToken orientation;
if (mesh.GetOrientationAttr().Get(&orientation) && orientation == UsdGeomTokens->leftHanded)
    for (size_t t = 0; t < out.indices.size(); t += 3)
    {
        std::swap(out.indices[t + 1], out.indices[t + 2]);
        std::swap(out.cornerFv[t + 1], out.cornerFv[t + 2]);
    }
@ Shading normals. A glossy highlight on a sphere made of flat facets shows
every facet, so we interpolate \emph{shading normals} across triangles when
the mesh is meant to look smooth. The rules follow what Pixar's Hydra renderers
do. Normals authored in the file win. Otherwise the subdivision scheme decides:
USD's default scheme is Catmull--Clark, meaning ``this cage describes a smooth
surface'', and we approximate that surface by smoothing the normals of the
unrefined cage; a mesh with [[subdivisionScheme = "none"]] is a true polygonal
mesh and is shaded flat. (The test scenes of this repository mark their boxes
and walls [[none]] for exactly this reason: with the default scheme a cube
shades like a rounded blob, which is correct USD behavior.)

<<Compute the shading normals of each triangle corner>>=
// Shading normals: authored primvars:normals or normals, otherwise smooth
// vertex normals for subdivision surfaces (as Hydra does) and flat for
// polygonal meshes.
<<Find the authored normals of a mesh>>
<<Compute smooth normals for a subdivision surface>>
<<Pick the shading normal of each triangle corner>>
@ Normals may be authored in two places. The primvar
\texttt{primvars:normals} takes precedence over the older [[normals]]
attribute, as the UsdGeom documentation prescribes. [[ComputeFlattened]]
expands an \emph{indexed} primvar (values plus an index array) into one value
per element, so we never deal with the indices ourselves.

<<Find the authored normals of a mesh>>=
VtArray<GfVec3f> normals;
TfToken interpolation;
if (const UsdGeomPrimvar pv = UsdGeomPrimvarsAPI(mesh).GetPrimvar(UsdGeomTokens->normals);
    pv && pv.ComputeFlattened(&normals, time))
    interpolation = pv.GetInterpolation();
else if (mesh.GetNormalsAttr().Get(&normals, time))
    interpolation = mesh.GetNormalsInterpolation();
@ Smooth normals are accumulated per point. The cross product of two edges of
a triangle has length twice its area, so summing the unnormalized cross
products weights each triangle by its area: large faces dominate, and a sliver
triangle does not tilt the normal. Since the triangles were rewound in the
previous step, all the cross products point outward. The sums are normalized
later, when the corners of the triangles are gathered.

<<Compute smooth normals for a subdivision surface>>=
TfToken scheme;
mesh.GetSubdivisionSchemeAttr().Get(&scheme);
if (normals.empty() && scheme != UsdGeomTokens->none)
{
    normals.assign(points.size(), GfVec3f(0.0f));
    for (size_t t = 0; t < out.indices.size(); t += 3)
    {
        const unsigned i0 = out.indices[t], i1 = out.indices[t + 1], i2 = out.indices[t + 2];
        const GfVec3f n = GfCross(points[i1] - points[i0], points[i2] - points[i0]);  // area weighted
        normals[i0] += n;
        normals[i1] += n;
        normals[i2] += n;
    }
    interpolation = UsdGeomTokens->vertex;
}
@ Finally each corner looks up its normal according to the interpolation:
by face-vertex index, by point index, or by face. A [[constant]] normal falls
through all the tests and uses element~0. An index beyond the array, which only
a malformed file produces, gives the zero vector; where all three corners of a
triangle carry it, the renderer falls back to the triangle's flat normal. When
there are no normals at all, [[cornerNormals]] stays empty and the whole mesh
is shaded flat.

<<Pick the shading normal of each triangle corner>>=
if (!normals.empty())
    for (size_t t = 0; t < out.indices.size(); t += 3)
        for (size_t c = 0; c < 3; ++c)
        {
            size_t i = 0;
            if (interpolation == UsdGeomTokens->faceVarying)
                i = out.cornerFv[t + c];
            else if (interpolation == UsdGeomTokens->vertex || interpolation == UsdGeomTokens->varying)
                i = out.indices[t + c];
            else if (interpolation == UsdGeomTokens->uniform)
                i = out.faceOfTri[t / 3];
            out.cornerNormals.push_back(i < normals.size() ? normals[i] : GfVec3f(0.0f));
        }
@ Implicit primitives. The cube and the sphere have no faces in USD's sense,
so after generating their triangles we fill in the bookkeeping: each corner's
``face-vertex'' index is simply its point index, and the whole primitive counts
as a single face, number~0. (These primitives carry no texture coordinates,
so the choice only has to be consistent.)

<<Close the bookkeeping of a generated primitive>>=
void finishPrimitive(LocalMesh& out)
{
    out.cornerFv = out.indices;
    out.faceOfTri.assign(out.indices.size() / 3, 0u);
}
@ The eight corners of a cube centered at the origin are numbered by three
bits: bit~0 selects $\pm x$, bit~1 $\pm y$ and bit~2 $\pm z$. The twelve
triangles, two per side, are listed so that each is counter-clockwise when
seen from outside; for instance $(0,2,1)$ lies on the $-z$ side, and
$(p_2-p_0)\times(p_1-p_0)$ is a positive multiple of $(0,1,0)\times(1,0,0)=
(0,0,-1)$, the outward direction. A cube has no shading normals: it is meant to
be flat.

<<Generate the triangles of a cube>>=
void meshFromCube(double size, LocalMesh& out)
{
    const float h = float(size) * 0.5f;
    for (int i = 0; i < 8; ++i)
        out.points.emplace_back(i & 1 ? h : -h, i & 2 ? h : -h, i & 4 ? h : -h);
    out.indices = {0, 2, 1, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 5, 7, 6, 0, 1, 4, 1, 5, 4,
                   2, 6, 3, 3, 6, 7, 0, 4, 2, 2, 4, 6, 1, 3, 5, 3, 7, 5};
    finishPrimitive(out);
}
@ The sphere is a latitude--longitude grid: 24 stacks from the north pole
($\phi=0$, on the $+y$ axis) to the south pole ($\phi=\pi$), and 48 slices
around. A point is
$$r\,(\sin\phi\cos\theta,\ \cos\phi,\ \sin\phi\sin\theta).$$
Each grid cell, with corners $a$ and $a+1$ on one stack and $b$ and $b+1$ on
the next, becomes two triangles. The order $(a, a+1, b)$ is the outward one:
near the equator at $\theta=0$, the edge from $a$ to $a+1$ runs toward $+z$
and the edge from $a$ to $b$ runs toward $-y$, and $(0,0,1)\times(0,-1,0) =
(1,0,0)$ points away from the center. The winding matters: shading normals
must agree with it.

On a sphere centered at the origin the normal at a point is the point itself,
scaled; we store the points as normals and let the gathering of the corners
normalize them. Every corner thus gets an exact, smooth normal. The grid
repeats points: the seam at $\theta=0$ and $\theta=2\pi$ has two copies of
each point, and each pole has 49. Welding, below, merges them back.

<<Generate the triangles of a sphere>>=
void meshFromSphere(double radius, LocalMesh& out)
{
    const int slices = 48, stacks = 24;
    for (int j = 0; j <= stacks; ++j)
    {
        const double phi = M_PI * j / stacks;
        for (int i = 0; i <= slices; ++i)
        {
            const double theta = 2.0 * M_PI * i / slices;
            out.points.emplace_back(float(radius * std::sin(phi) * std::cos(theta)), float(radius * std::cos(phi)),
                                    float(radius * std::sin(phi) * std::sin(theta)));
        }
    }
    for (int j = 0; j < stacks; ++j)
        for (int i = 0; i < slices; ++i)
        {
            const unsigned a = j * (slices + 1) + i, b = a + slices + 1;
            out.indices.insert(out.indices.end(), {a, a + 1, b, a + 1, b + 1, b});  // counter-clockwise from outside
        }
    finishPrimitive(out);
    for (unsigned idx : out.indices)
        out.cornerNormals.push_back(out.points[idx]);  // normalized later
}
@* Materials. The viewer understands a subset of \emph{UsdPreviewSurface},
the simple physically based material that every USD tool can read. A material
in USD is a small graph: a [[Material]] prim whose surface output is connected
to a [[UsdPreviewSurface]] shader, whose inputs are either values or
connections to other shaders, typically [[UsdUVTexture]] nodes, which in turn
read their texture coordinates from [[UsdPrimvarReader_float2]] nodes. We
condense such a graph into a flat [[Material]] record that the GPU can read
without following any pointers.

The first kind of record field is used for decoded images. A [[Texture]] holds
eight-bit RGBA pixels, whether they should be converted from sRGB to linear
values when sampled, and what happens outside the unit square of texture
coordinates in each direction. USD's [[UsdUVTexture]] offers repeat, mirror,
clamp and black, which map directly to CUDA's address modes.

<<Texture wrap modes and decoded textures>>=
// ---- render data ----------------------------------------------------------
enum class TextureWrap { Repeat, Mirror, Clamp, Black };

struct Texture
{
    std::string path;  // resolved path (may point inside a .usdz)
    int width = 0, height = 0;
    std::vector<unsigned char> rgba;
    bool srgb = true;
    TextureWrap wrapS = TextureWrap::Repeat, wrapT = TextureWrap::Repeat;
};
@ Several inputs of UsdPreviewSurface, such as roughness and metallic, are
single numbers that may instead come from one channel of a texture. Packing
several such maps into the channels of one image is common practice, so we
record which channel to read, and that channel's scale and bias from the
[[UsdUVTexture]] node, which maps the texel $t$ to $t\cdot\mathit{scale}+
\mathit{bias}$.

<<The ScalarInput material parameter>>=
// A float material input: a constant, or one channel of a texture
// (texture[channel] * scale + bias).
struct ScalarInput
{
    float value = 0.0f;
    int texture = -1;
    int channel = 0;
    float scale = 1.0f, bias = 0.0f;
};
@ The [[Material]] record. Texture fields are indices into the scene's
[[textures]], with $-1$ for ``none''.

<<The Material record>>=
// Simplified UsdPreviewSurface: diffuse color, optionally from a texture
// (texture * scale + bias), tangent-space normal maps, alpha cutouts
// (opacity + opacityThreshold) and GGX specular in the metallic or the
// specular workflow.
struct Material
{
    <<Diffuse, cutout and normal-map fields of a material>>

    <<Specular fields of a material>>

    <<Test whether a material reads any texture>>
};
@ The name is for the inspector. The diffuse color defaults to a light gray,
which is what a prim without any material or display color looks like. The
texture coordinates are read from the primvar named by [[uvSet]], almost
always [[st]]. A cutout discards hits where one channel of a texture, usually
alpha, falls below a threshold. The defaults of the normal-map remapping,
$2t-1$, turn the usual eight-bit encoding of a unit vector, with components
in $[0,1]$, back into components in $[-1,1]$.

<<Diffuse, cutout and normal-map fields of a material>>=
std::string name;  // material prim path, or "displayColor"
std::array<float, 3> color = {0.7f, 0.7f, 0.7f};
int texture = -1;
std::array<float, 4> scale = {1, 1, 1, 1};
std::array<float, 4> bias = {0, 0, 0, 0};
std::string uvSet = "st";  // primvar read by the texture
// alpha cutout: hits where opacityTexture[opacityChannel] < opacityThreshold are ignored
int opacityTexture = -1;
int opacityChannel = 3;
float opacityThreshold = 0.0f;
// tangent-space normal = normalTexture * normalScale + normalBias
int normalTexture = -1;
std::array<float, 4> normalScale = {2, 2, 2, 1};
std::array<float, 4> normalBias = {-1, -1, -1, 0};
@ UsdPreviewSurface has two ways of describing the specular reflection. In
the default \emph{metallic workflow}, a metallic factor blends between a
dielectric, whose reflectance at normal incidence follows from the index of
refraction, and a metal, which reflects with its diffuse color. In the
\emph{specular workflow} the reflectance at normal incidence is given directly
as a color. The defaults are those of the specification: roughness~0.5,
metallic~0, index of refraction~1.5.

<<Specular fields of a material>>=
ScalarInput roughness{0.5f};
ScalarInput metallic{0.0f};
float ior = 1.5f;
// useSpecularWorkflow: specularColor replaces metallic as the reflectance at normal incidence
bool specularWorkflow = false;
std::array<float, 3> specularColor = {0, 0, 0};
int specularTexture = -1;
std::array<float, 4> specularScale = {1, 1, 1, 1};
std::array<float, 4> specularBias = {0, 0, 0, 0};
@ A material that reads any texture needs texture coordinates. This question
is asked when choosing which primvar to read and when deciding whether to warn
about missing coordinates.

<<Test whether a material reads any texture>>=
bool usesTextures() const
{
    return texture >= 0 || opacityTexture >= 0 || normalTexture >= 0 || roughness.texture >= 0 ||
           metallic.texture >= 0 || specularTexture >= 0;
}
@ Reading shading networks. UsdShade connections can be indirect: a shader
input may be connected to an input of the enclosing [[Material]] (its
\emph{interface}), which may in turn be connected to something else, possibly
through node graphs. [[GetValueProducingAttributes]] follows such chains to
their ends and returns the attributes that actually produce the value: either
outputs of shaders, or inputs that hold a value. We want a plain value, so we
accept only the latter. This is how a texture whose file name is published on
the material's interface, as in our test scene, still finds its file.

The function is a template because inputs come in many types: colors, floats,
integers, tokens, asset paths. When the input exists but holds a value of
another type, [[Get]] fails and so do we, and the caller keeps its default.

<<Read the value of a shader input>>=
// Value of a shader input, following connections to material interface
// inputs. Returns false when the input is unset or driven by another shader.
template <typename T>
bool inputValue(const UsdShadeShader& shader, const char* name, T* value)
{
    const UsdShadeInput input = shader.GetInput(TfToken(name));
    if (!input)
        return false;
    for (const UsdAttribute& attr : input.GetValueProducingAttributes())
        if (UsdShadeUtils::GetType(attr.GetName()) == UsdShadeAttributeType::Input && attr.Get(value))
            return true;
    return false;
}
@ The other question to ask of an input is ``which shader drives it?''. With
[[shaderOutputsOnly]] set, the same function returns only shader outputs; we
take the first. Callers sometimes need the output itself, because its name
([[outputs:rgb]], [[outputs:r]], \ldots) says which channels are used, and
sometimes only the shader.

<<Find the shader that drives an input>>=
// Shader output that drives the given input (through node graphs), if any.
UsdAttribute sourceOutput(const UsdShadeInput& input)
{
    if (input)
        for (const UsdAttribute& attr : input.GetValueProducingAttributes(/*shaderOutputsOnly=*/true))
            return attr;
    return UsdAttribute();
}

UsdShadeShader sourceShader(const UsdShadeInput& input)
{
    const UsdAttribute output = sourceOutput(input);
    return output ? UsdShadeShader(output.GetPrim()) : UsdShadeShader();
}
@ A shader's kind is its [[info:id]], such as [[UsdUVTexture]]. The wrap mode
[[useMetadata]], USD's default, asks to use whatever the image file says; the
formats we decode carry no such metadata, so it is treated as [[repeat]], like
anything unrecognized.

<<Identify a shader and its wrap modes>>=
TfToken shaderId(const UsdShadeShader& shader)
{
    TfToken id;
    shader.GetShaderId(&id);
    return id;
}

TextureWrap wrapMode(const UsdShadeShader& shader, const char* name)
{
    TfToken mode;
    inputValue(shader, name, &mode);
    if (mode == "mirror")
        return TextureWrap::Mirror;
    if (mode == "clamp")
        return TextureWrap::Clamp;
    if (mode == "black")
        return TextureWrap::Black;
    return TextureWrap::Repeat;  // "repeat" and "useMetadata"
}
@ A prim without a bound material is drawn with its \emph{display color}, a
primvar that every gprim may carry, and with all other properties at their
defaults. Display colors differ from prim to prim, so these materials are not
cached; each such prim gets its own record. A display color may vary over the
surface; we use its first value only.

<<Material for a prim without a bound material>>=
// ---- materials and textures -------------------------------------------------
unsigned int Scene::displayColorMaterial(const UsdPrim& prim)
{
    Material m;
    m.name = "displayColor";
    VtArray<GfVec3f> colors;
    if (UsdGeomGprim(prim).GetDisplayColorPrimvar().ComputeFlattened(&colors, m_time) && !colors.empty())
        m.color = {colors[0][0], colors[0][1], colors[0][2]};
    m_materials.push_back(m);
    return unsigned(m_materials.size() - 1);
}
@ Converting a UsdShade material proceeds input by input.
[[ComputeSurfaceSource]] finds the shader connected to the material's surface
output. A material without one keeps the defaults; one with a surface shader
has its inputs read one group at a time.

<<Convert a UsdShade material into a Material record>>=
unsigned int Scene::materialFor(const UsdShadeMaterial& material)
{
    <<Return the cached Material of a UsdShade material>>

    <<Start a Material with the UsdPreviewSurface defaults>>
    if (const UsdShadeShader surface = material.ComputeSurfaceSource())
    {
        <<Read the diffuse color of a surface>>

        <<Read the diffuse texture of a surface>>

        <<Read an alpha cutout of a surface>>

        <<Read the specular parameters of a surface>>

        <<Read the normal map of a surface>>
    }
    <<Store a new Material in the cache>>
}
@ Many prims usually share a material, so the conversion is cached by the
path of the material prim.

<<Return the cached Material of a UsdShade material>>=
const SdfPath path = material.GetPath();
if (auto it = m_materialCache.find(path); it != m_materialCache.end())
    return it->second;
@ A material that does bind but specifies nothing is a UsdPreviewSurface
with all inputs at their defaults, and the default diffuse color of the
specification is a dark gray, $0.18$.

<<Start a Material with the UsdPreviewSurface defaults>>=
Material m;
m.name = path.GetString();
m.color = {0.18f, 0.18f, 0.18f};  // UsdPreviewSurface default
@
<<Store a new Material in the cache>>=
m_materials.push_back(m);
const unsigned index = unsigned(m_materials.size() - 1);
m_materialCache[path] = index;
return index;
@ The diffuse color. A plain value, possibly published on the material's
interface, is the easy case. But an input can carry both a value and a
connection: many exporters write the color that a texture would average to
next to the connection to the texture. [[inputValue]] does not return that
value, because the connection wins, so we read the attribute's own value
separately. It becomes the color used if the texture cannot be loaded, so a
textured model whose image is missing still looks plausible.

<<Read the diffuse color of a surface>>=
GfVec3f c;
if (inputValue(surface, "diffuseColor", &c))
    m.color = {c[0], c[1], c[2]};
else if (const UsdShadeInput in = surface.GetInput(TfToken("diffuseColor")); in && in.GetAttr().Get(&c))
    m.color = {c[0], c[1], c[2]};  // authored default beside a connection
@ If [[diffuseColor]] is driven by a [[UsdUVTexture]], the record points to
the decoded image, and the texture's coordinates tell us which primvar to
read. Any other kind of shader (a procedural, or a MaterialX node) is beyond
us, and we say so.

<<Read the diffuse texture of a surface>>=
const UsdShadeShader texture = sourceShader(surface.GetInput(TfToken("diffuseColor")));
if (texture && shaderId(texture) == "UsdUVTexture")
{
    <<Read the scale, bias and fallback of the diffuse texture>>

    <<Find the primvar that holds the diffuse texture coordinates>>
}
else if (texture)
    warn("Unsupported shader driving diffuseColor: " + shaderId(texture).GetString() + " (" + m.name + ")");
@ The texture node's own [[fallback]] value, meant for when the image is
missing, takes precedence over the color found above. Diffuse textures are
decoded as sRGB unless the node says otherwise, which is what nearly every
color image is.

<<Read the scale, bias and fallback of the diffuse texture>>=
GfVec4f v;
if (inputValue(texture, "scale", &v))
    m.scale = {v[0], v[1], v[2], v[3]};
if (inputValue(texture, "bias", &v))
    m.bias = {v[0], v[1], v[2], v[3]};
if (inputValue(texture, "fallback", &v))
    m.color = {v[0], v[1], v[2]};
m.texture = loadTexture(texture);
@ The [[st]] input of the texture is normally connected to a primvar reader,
whose [[varname]] names the primvar. The specification says [[varname]] is a
string, but older files, and some current exporters, write a token; we accept
both. A [[UsdTransform2d]] node between the reader and the texture would
scale, rotate or offset the coordinates; we do not implement it, and the
coordinates are then read, untransformed, from the default primvar [[st]].

<<Find the primvar that holds the diffuse texture coordinates>>=
// texture coordinates come from a UsdPrimvarReader_float2
const UsdShadeShader reader = sourceShader(texture.GetInput(TfToken("st")));
VtValue varname;
if (reader && shaderId(reader) == "UsdTransform2d")
    warn("UsdTransform2d texture transforms are ignored (" + m.name + ")");
else if (reader && inputValue(reader, "varname", &varname))
{
    if (varname.IsHolding<TfToken>())
        m.uvSet = varname.UncheckedGet<TfToken>().GetString();
    else if (varname.IsHolding<std::string>())
        m.uvSet = varname.UncheckedGet<std::string>();
}
@ Alpha cutouts. UsdPreviewSurface treats opacity in two ways. With an
[[opacityThreshold]] greater than zero, a point is either fully present (opacity
at or above the threshold) or fully absent: this is how leaves, fences and
grass blades are cut out of simple quads. With a zero threshold, opacity means
partial transparency, which a ray tracer could do but this one does not; such
materials are drawn opaque, with a warning. Only an opacity that comes from a
texture can cut anything out, so a constant opacity is ignored. The channel is
named by the texture output the input is connected to: [[outputs:a]] for alpha,
or one of the color channels.

<<Read an alpha cutout of a surface>>=
// alpha cutout from a texture channel (translucency is not supported)
float threshold = 0.0f;
inputValue(surface, "opacityThreshold", &threshold);
const UsdAttribute opacity = sourceOutput(surface.GetInput(TfToken("opacity")));
if (opacity && shaderId(UsdShadeShader(opacity.GetPrim())) == "UsdUVTexture")
{
    if (threshold > 0.0f)
    {
        const std::string channel = UsdShadeUtils::GetBaseNameAndType(opacity.GetName()).first.GetString();
        m.opacityTexture = loadTexture(UsdShadeShader(opacity.GetPrim()));
        m.opacityChannel = channel == "r" ? 0 : channel == "g" ? 1 : channel == "b" ? 2 : 3;
        m.opacityThreshold = threshold;
    }
    else
        warn("Translucent materials are rendered opaque (" + m.name + ")");
}
@ The specular parameters. Roughness and metallic are read by
<<Read a float input that may come from a texture>>, the index of refraction
is a plain number, and [[useSpecularWorkflow]] is an integer flag. In the
specular workflow the [[specularColor]] may be a constant or a color texture,
with the texture's scale and bias.

<<Read the specular parameters of a surface>>=
// specular: metallic or specular workflow
m.roughness = scalarInput(surface, "roughness", 0.5f);
m.metallic = scalarInput(surface, "metallic", 0.0f);
inputValue(surface, "ior", &m.ior);
int useSpecularWorkflow = 0;
inputValue(surface, "useSpecularWorkflow", &useSpecularWorkflow);
m.specularWorkflow = useSpecularWorkflow != 0;
if (m.specularWorkflow)
{
    GfVec3f c;
    if (inputValue(surface, "specularColor", &c))
        m.specularColor = {c[0], c[1], c[2]};
    const UsdShadeShader specular = sourceShader(surface.GetInput(TfToken("specularColor")));
    if (specular && shaderId(specular) == "UsdUVTexture")
    {
        m.specularTexture = loadTexture(specular);
        GfVec4f v;
        if (inputValue(specular, "scale", &v))
            m.specularScale = {v[0], v[1], v[2], v[3]};
        if (inputValue(specular, "bias", &v))
            m.specularBias = {v[0], v[1], v[2], v[3]};
    }
}
@ Normal maps. A tangent-space normal map stores, in each texel, a direction
relative to the surface: $x$ along the direction of increasing $s$, $y$ along
increasing $t$, $z$ out of the surface. The texel values are data, not colors,
so the image is decoded \emph{raw} unless the file insists on sRGB.

<<Read the normal map of a surface>>=
// tangent-space normal map
const UsdShadeShader normal = sourceShader(surface.GetInput(TfToken("normal")));
if (normal && shaderId(normal) == "UsdUVTexture")
{
    m.normalTexture = loadTexture(normal, /*srgbByDefault=*/false);
    <<Decide how to decode the normal map>>
    <<Find the primvar that holds the normal-map coordinates>>
}
else if (normal)
    warn("Unsupported shader driving normal: " + shaderId(normal).GetString() + " (" + m.name + ")");
@ The specification asks files to say, through the texture's scale and bias,
how texels become vectors: for eight-bit images, scale $(2,2,2,1)$ and bias
$(-1,-1,-1,0)$. We follow what is authored exactly. This is not pedantry:
normal maps made for DirectX have the $y$ axis flipped, and well-made USD files
compensate with scale $(2,-2,2)$ and bias $(-1,1,-1)$, as the McUsd sample
asset does. If only one of the two is authored, the other keeps the
[[UsdUVTexture]] default. If neither is authored, the file is not compliant
(\texttt{usdchecker} complains about it), but such files are common, so rather
than read the vectors as nonsense we assume the usual remapping and warn.

<<Decide how to decode the normal map>>=
GfVec4f scale, bias;
const bool hasScale = inputValue(normal, "scale", &scale), hasBias = inputValue(normal, "bias", &bias);
if (hasScale || hasBias)
{
    // as authored; UsdUVTexture defaults are scale 1, bias 0
    m.normalScale = hasScale ? std::array<float, 4>{scale[0], scale[1], scale[2], scale[3]}
                             : std::array<float, 4>{1, 1, 1, 1};
    m.normalBias = hasBias ? std::array<float, 4>{bias[0], bias[1], bias[2], bias[3]}
                           : std::array<float, 4>{0, 0, 0, 0};
}
else
    warn("Normal map without scale/bias, assuming the usual 2x-1 remap (" + m.name + ")");
@ A material has one [[uvSet]]. When it has a diffuse texture, that texture
has already chosen it; otherwise the normal map's primvar reader decides.

<<Find the primvar that holds the normal-map coordinates>>=
// 'uvSet' is shared with the diffuse texture when there is one
if (m.texture < 0)
    if (const UsdShadeShader reader = sourceShader(normal.GetInput(TfToken("st"))))
    {
        VtValue varname;
        if (inputValue(reader, "varname", &varname))
        {
            if (varname.IsHolding<TfToken>())
                m.uvSet = varname.UncheckedGet<TfToken>().GetString();
            else if (varname.IsHolding<std::string>())
                m.uvSet = varname.UncheckedGet<std::string>();
        }
    }
@ A float input such as roughness starts at its default, then takes the
authored value, if any. If it is driven by a texture, we record which channel:
the output [[outputs:g]] means green, and so on; [[outputs:r]] and anything
else, such as [[outputs:rgb]], mean the first channel. Like normal maps,
roughness and metallic maps are data and are decoded raw by default. The
texture's scale and bias are reduced to those of the chosen channel, and if
the image cannot be loaded, the texture's [[fallback]] for that channel
replaces the constant.

<<Read a float input that may come from a texture>>=
ScalarInput Scene::scalarInput(const UsdShadeShader& surface, const char* name, float fallback)
{
    ScalarInput in;
    in.value = fallback;
    inputValue(surface, name, &in.value);
    const UsdAttribute output = sourceOutput(surface.GetInput(TfToken(name)));
    if (!output)
        return in;
    const UsdShadeShader texture(output.GetPrim());
    if (shaderId(texture) != "UsdUVTexture")
    {
        warn("Unsupported shader driving " + std::string(name) + ": " + shaderId(texture).GetString());
        return in;
    }
    const std::string channel = UsdShadeUtils::GetBaseNameAndType(output.GetName()).first.GetString();
    in.channel = channel == "g" ? 1 : channel == "b" ? 2 : channel == "a" ? 3 : 0;
    in.texture = loadTexture(texture, /*srgbByDefault=*/false);
    GfVec4f v;
    if (inputValue(texture, "scale", &v))
        in.scale = v[in.channel];
    if (inputValue(texture, "bias", &v))
        in.bias = v[in.channel];
    if (in.texture < 0 && inputValue(texture, "fallback", &v))
        in.value = v[in.channel];
    return in;
}
@* Textures. Loading a texture turns a [[UsdUVTexture]] node into an index
into the scene's [[textures]], or $-1$ with a warning if anything goes wrong.

<<Load a texture once>>=
int Scene::loadTexture(const UsdShadeShader& shader, bool srgbByDefault)
{
    <<Resolve the file of a texture>>

    <<Decide the color space and wrap modes, and consult the texture cache>>

    <<Decode a texture through the asset resolver>>
}
@ The file is an \emph{asset path}. USD resolves asset paths when it composes
the stage, relative to the layer that authored them, so
[[GetResolvedPath]] already gives an absolute location. For a file inside a
\lpfile{.usdz} package, that location has the form
\lpfile{package.usdz[textures/image.png]}, which no ordinary file function can
open; we shall see in a moment how it is read. UDIM textures, whose name
contains the pattern \texttt{<UDIM>} and stands for a whole set of images, one
per tile of texture space, are not supported.

<<Resolve the file of a texture>>=
SdfAssetPath file;
if (!inputValue(shader, "file", &file) || file.GetAssetPath().empty())
{
    warn("Texture without a file: " + shader.GetPath().GetString());
    return -1;
}
if (file.GetAssetPath().find("<UDIM>") != std::string::npos)
{
    warn("UDIM textures are not supported: " + file.GetAssetPath());
    return -1;
}
const std::string resolved = file.GetResolvedPath();
if (resolved.empty())
{
    warn("Texture not found: " + file.GetAssetPath());
    return -1;
}
@ The color space. [[sourceColorSpace]] may be [[sRGB]], [[raw]] or [[auto]]
(the default). An explicit choice is obeyed. For [[auto]] the decision should
depend on the image; we let it depend on the use instead, which is the same
thing in practice: colors are sRGB, data (normals, roughness, metallic) are
raw. The caller says which.

The renderer turns each [[Texture]] into a CUDA texture object, in which both
the sRGB conversion and the wrap modes are fixed. The same image read in two
different ways is therefore two textures, and the cache key contains all four
properties: resolved path, color space and both wrap modes.

<<Decide the color space and wrap modes, and consult the texture cache>>=
TfToken colorSpace;
inputValue(shader, "sourceColorSpace", &colorSpace);
const bool srgb = colorSpace == "sRGB" || (colorSpace != "raw" && srgbByDefault);
const TextureWrap wrapS = wrapMode(shader, "wrapS"), wrapT = wrapMode(shader, "wrapT");
const auto key = std::make_tuple(resolved, srgb, int(wrapS), int(wrapT));
if (auto it = m_textureCache.find(key); it != m_textureCache.end())
    return it->second;
@ The asset resolver, \emph{Ar}, is USD's layer between asset paths and
bytes. Asking it to open the resolved path works for plain files and for files
inside packages alike, and gives us the whole file in memory, which is exactly
what the decoder \texttt{stb\_image} wants. We ask the decoder for four
channels of eight bits whatever the file contains; grayscale and RGB images
gain the missing channels, with an opaque alpha. Failures are remembered in
the cache as well, so a missing or broken image is reported once, not once per
material that reads it.

<<Decode a texture through the asset resolver>>=
// ArResolver also reads files packaged inside .usdz archives
int index = -1;
std::shared_ptr<ArAsset> asset = ArGetResolver().OpenAsset(ArResolvedPath(resolved));
std::shared_ptr<const char> buffer = asset ? asset->GetBuffer() : nullptr;
int w = 0, h = 0, channels = 0;
unsigned char* pixels =
    buffer ? stbi_load_from_memory(reinterpret_cast<const stbi_uc*>(buffer.get()), int(asset->GetSize()), &w, &h,
                                   &channels, 4)
           : nullptr;
if (pixels)
{
    Texture t;
    t.path = resolved;
    t.width = w;
    t.height = h;
    t.rgba.assign(pixels, pixels + size_t(w) * h * 4);
    t.srgb = srgb;
    t.wrapS = wrapS;
    t.wrapT = wrapT;
    stbi_image_free(pixels);
    m_textures.push_back(std::move(t));
    index = int(m_textures.size() - 1);
}
else
    warn("Could not load texture " + resolved + (buffer ? std::string(": ") + stbi_failure_reason() : ""));
m_textureCache[key] = index;
return index;
@* Materials per face. Back in the extraction loop, each prim needs a
material for each of its faces. The material bound to the prim is found by
[[ComputeBoundMaterial]], which applies USD's binding rules for us: bindings
are inherited from ancestors, and a binding marked ``stronger than
descendants'' overrides those below it. A prim with no material falls back to
its display color.

A mesh may also be divided into \emph{GeomSubsets}, named sets of faces, and
the subsets of the family [[materialBind]] can carry their own material
binding. Those faces get the subset's material instead; indices outside the
mesh are ignored. The count of such subsets is shown in the inspector.

<<Assign a material to every face of a prim>>=
// materials: the prim's own binding, overridden per face by GeomSubsets
UsdShadeMaterialBindingAPI binding(p);
const UsdShadeMaterial primMaterial = binding.ComputeBoundMaterial();
const unsigned baseMaterial = primMaterial ? materialFor(primMaterial) : displayColorMaterial(p);
std::vector<unsigned> faceMaterial(local.faceCount, baseMaterial);
unsigned subsetMaterials = 0;
for (const UsdGeomSubset& subset : binding.GetMaterialBindSubsets())
{
    const UsdShadeMaterial m = UsdShadeMaterialBindingAPI(subset.GetPrim()).ComputeBoundMaterial();
    VtIntArray faces;
    if (!m || !subset.GetIndicesAttr().Get(&faces, m_time))
        continue;
    const unsigned index = materialFor(m);
    for (int f : faces)
        if (f >= 0 && size_t(f) < faceMaterial.size())
            faceMaterial[f] = index;
    ++subsetMaterials;
}
@ Texture coordinates. Each prim gets one set of texture coordinates per
triangle corner, read from one primvar. Which primvar? The one the material
asks for: the prim's own material, unless some face uses a textured material,
in which case the first such material decides. Prims whose subsets read
different primvars are not handled, but they are rare.

Primvars can be inherited: a primvar authored with [[constant]] interpolation
on an ancestor applies to the whole subtree, which is why we use
[[FindPrimvarWithInheritance]]. As with normals, [[ComputeFlattened]] resolves
indexed primvars, and the lambda [[cornerUV]] picks the value of each corner by
the same rules as <<Pick the shading normal of each triangle corner>>. A
missing value gives the coordinates $(0,0)$.

<<Read the texture coordinates of a prim>>=
// texture coordinates, read from the primvar the material asks for
std::string uvSet = m_materials[baseMaterial].uvSet;
for (unsigned m : faceMaterial)
    if (m_materials[m].usesTextures())
    {
        uvSet = m_materials[m].uvSet;
        break;
    }
VtArray<GfVec2f> uvs;
TfToken interpolation;
if (const UsdGeomPrimvar pv = UsdGeomPrimvarsAPI(p).FindPrimvarWithInheritance(TfToken(uvSet));
    pv && pv.ComputeFlattened(&uvs, m_time))
    interpolation = pv.GetInterpolation();
auto cornerUV = [&](unsigned tri, int corner) -> GfVec2f {
    size_t i = 0;
    if (interpolation == UsdGeomTokens->faceVarying)
        i = local.cornerFv[3 * tri + corner];
    else if (interpolation == UsdGeomTokens->vertex || interpolation == UsdGeomTokens->varying)
        i = local.indices[3 * tri + corner];
    else if (interpolation == UsdGeomTokens->uniform)
        i = local.faceOfTri[tri];
    return i < uvs.size() ? uvs[i] : GfVec2f(0.0f);
};
@* Corners and vertices. A GPU vertex carries one value of every attribute:
one position, one normal, one pair of texture coordinates. USD's points are
not such vertices. A cube whose faces are each mapped to the whole texture
has eight points, but the corner of three faces meets them with three
different texture coordinates, and the same holds for the normals of a
hard edge. The face-varying attributes make the \emph{corner} of a face the
natural unit, so we first spell out every corner of every triangle, with its
position, its shading normal and its texture coordinates, all in local space,
and leave it to the next step to find which corners are really the same
vertex.

Normals are normalized here, which also takes care of the unnormalized sums
of the smooth normals and of the sphere's points. Normalizing before welding
matters: two corners whose normals point the same way but have different
lengths would otherwise remain distinct vertices. A degenerate normal is left
as it is. The material of each triangle is that of its source face. Only the
attributes the prim has are gathered, so [[normals]] and [[cornerUVs]] may
stay empty.

<<Gather the corners of a prim's triangles>>=
// every triangle corner with its attributes, in the prim's local space
const unsigned triCount = unsigned(local.indices.size() / 3);
std::vector<float> positions, normals, cornerUVs;
std::vector<unsigned> triMaterial(triCount);
for (unsigned t = 0; t < triCount; ++t)
{
    triMaterial[t] = faceMaterial[local.faceOfTri[t]];
    for (int c = 0; c < 3; ++c)
    {
        const GfVec3f& pt = local.points[local.indices[3 * t + c]];
        positions.insert(positions.end(), {pt[0], pt[1], pt[2]});
        if (!local.cornerNormals.empty())
        {
            GfVec3f n = local.cornerNormals[3 * t + c];
            if (n.GetLength() > 1e-12f)
                n.Normalize();
            normals.insert(normals.end(), {n[0], n[1], n[2]});
        }
        if (!uvs.empty())
        {
            const GfVec2f uv = cornerUV(t, c);
            cornerUVs.insert(cornerUVs.end(), {uv[0], uv[1]});
        }
    }
}
@ The mesh record is started with what the inspector shows, and the corners
are handed to [[weldCorners]], which fills in the vertices, the triangles and
their materials.

<<Build the indexed mesh of a prim>>=
RenderMesh mesh;
mesh.path = p.GetPath();
mesh.material = baseMaterial;
mesh.subsetMaterials = subsetMaterials;
weldCorners(std::move(positions), std::move(normals), std::move(cornerUVs), triMaterial, mesh);
@ Welding. Corners with the same position, the same normal and the same
texture coordinates are the same vertex, and should be stored once: on a
smooth mesh, a vertex is typically shared by six triangles, so the vertex
arrays shrink to about a sixth of the corner arrays. Corners that differ in
any attribute, like the three corners of a textured cube at one point, stay
separate, which is exactly what the face-varying data require. The tool for
this is \emph{meshoptimizer}, a library of mesh-processing algorithms by
Arseny Kapoulkine. Along the way it reorders the triangles and the vertices
for memory locality, for reasons explained in the sections that follow. The
arguments are taken by value, so that the caller can move its arrays in and
the function can reorder them in place.

<<Weld the corners of a prim into an indexed mesh>>=
// Turns per-corner attributes (3 corners per triangle) into an indexed mesh
// with meshoptimizer. Corners with the same position, normal and st become one
// vertex. The triangles are grouped by material and sorted for spatial
// locality within each group, and the vertices are stored in the order the
// triangles first use them, so a ray's hits touch nearby memory.
void weldCorners(std::vector<float> positions, std::vector<float> normals, std::vector<float> uvs,
                 const std::vector<unsigned>& triMaterial, RenderMesh& out)
{
    <<Find the distinct corners of a mesh>>
    <<Reorder one attribute stream through a remap table>>
    <<Replace the corners by the distinct vertices>>

    <<Group the triangles by material>>
    <<Sort the triangles of each material spatially>>

    <<Store the vertices in the order the triangles first use them>>
}
@ The corners form an unindexed vertex buffer, three per triangle, split
into separate \emph{streams}, one per attribute. Given the streams,
[[meshopt_generateVertexRemapMulti]] compares corners bit for bit across all
of them, and fills [[remap]] with the new index of each corner: equal corners
get the same index, and the indices count up from zero without gaps in the
order of first appearance. It returns the number of distinct vertices. A null
index buffer means ``the corners are in order''.

<<Find the distinct corners of a mesh>>=
const size_t corners = positions.size() / 3, triCount = corners / 3;
std::vector<meshopt_Stream> streams = {{positions.data(), 3 * sizeof(float), 3 * sizeof(float)}};
if (!normals.empty())
    streams.push_back({normals.data(), 3 * sizeof(float), 3 * sizeof(float)});
if (!uvs.empty())
    streams.push_back({uvs.data(), 2 * sizeof(float), 2 * sizeof(float)});
std::vector<unsigned> remap(corners);
const size_t vertexCount =
    meshopt_generateVertexRemapMulti(remap.data(), nullptr, corners, corners, streams.data(), streams.size());
@ Every attribute array must follow every reordering of the vertices, so a
small helper applies a remap table to one stream: it builds an array of [[to]]
vertices from one of [[from]] vertices, each element made of [[floats]]
floats, and swaps it in. An attribute the prim does not have is left empty.

<<Reorder one attribute stream through a remap table>>=
auto remapStream = [&](std::vector<float>& stream, size_t floats, size_t from, size_t to,
                       const std::vector<unsigned>& table) {
    if (stream.empty())
        return;
    std::vector<float> result(to * floats);
    meshopt_remapVertexBuffer(result.data(), stream.data(), from, floats * sizeof(float), table.data());
    stream.swap(result);
};
@ The triangles become an index buffer, [[welded]], whose entry for each
corner is that corner's new vertex index, and the three attribute streams
shrink to the distinct vertices.

<<Replace the corners by the distinct vertices>>=
std::vector<unsigned> welded(corners);
meshopt_remapIndexBuffer(welded.data(), nullptr, corners, remap.data());
remapStream(positions, 3, corners, vertexCount, remap);
remapStream(normals, 3, corners, vertexCount, remap);
remapStream(uvs, 2, corners, vertexCount, remap);
@ Reordering the triangles. The order of the triangles in a GAS is free:
OptiX builds its own hierarchy over them anyway. But each triangle carries its
material in [[triMaterial]], indexed by the triangle's position, so whatever
order we choose, the materials must move with the triangles. We first sort
the triangles by material, with a stable sort so that the source order
survives within each material, and copy the indices and the materials in that
order together. Grouping by material makes the next step possible: a spatial
sort of each group on its own leaves every triangle in its group, and
[[triMaterial]] stays aligned with the triangle order.

<<Group the triangles by material>>=
std::vector<unsigned> order(triCount);
std::iota(order.begin(), order.end(), 0u);
std::stable_sort(order.begin(), order.end(),
                 [&](unsigned a, unsigned b) { return triMaterial[a] < triMaterial[b]; });
std::vector<unsigned> sorted(corners);
out.triMaterial.resize(triCount);
for (size_t t = 0; t < triCount; ++t)
{
    std::copy_n(&welded[3 * size_t(order[t])], 3, &sorted[3 * t]);
    out.triMaterial[t] = triMaterial[order[t]];
}
@ Within each run of triangles with the same material,
[[meshopt_spatialSortTriangles]] orders the triangles along a space-filling
curve through their centroids, so that triangles close in space are close in
the index buffer. A ray tracer reads memory in an order dictated by space: a
ray, and the rays of its neighboring pixels, hit triangles that lie near each
other. When those triangles are also near each other in memory, their
indices, materials and vertices share cache lines, on the GPU and in the
acceleration structure built over them. The sort works in place.

<<Sort the triangles of each material spatially>>=
for (size_t begin = 0, end; begin < triCount; begin = end)
{
    for (end = begin + 1; end < triCount && out.triMaterial[end] == out.triMaterial[begin]; ++end)
        ;
    meshopt_spatialSortTriangles(&sorted[3 * begin], &sorted[3 * begin], 3 * (end - begin), positions.data(),
                                 vertexCount, 3 * sizeof(float));
}
@ The vertices still have the order in which their corners first appeared
in the source, which does not match the sorted triangles. [[meshopt_optimizeVertexFetchRemap]]
renumbers them in the order in which the sorted triangles first use them, so
that walking the triangles walks the vertex arrays almost sequentially, and
the vertices of neighboring triangles are neighbors in memory too. It returns
the number of vertices used, which equals the number of distinct vertices,
since every vertex comes from some corner. The final index buffer and the
attribute streams are rewritten through this remap and moved into the mesh,
together with the number of corners the mesh started with.

<<Store the vertices in the order the triangles first use them>>=
std::vector<unsigned> fetch(vertexCount);
const size_t used = meshopt_optimizeVertexFetchRemap(fetch.data(), sorted.data(), corners, vertexCount);
out.indices.resize(corners);
meshopt_remapIndexBuffer(out.indices.data(), sorted.data(), corners, fetch.data());
remapStream(positions, 3, vertexCount, used, fetch);
remapStream(normals, 3, vertexCount, used, fetch);
remapStream(uvs, 2, vertexCount, used, fetch);
out.positions = std::move(positions);
out.normals = std::move(normals);
out.uvs = std::move(uvs);
out.corners = corners;
@* World space. The mesh itself stays in local space; what goes to the
renderer is the local-to-world matrix $M$ of the prim. The two libraries
write matrices differently. USD's matrices act on row vectors: a point is
transformed as $p' = pM$, so the translation is the last \emph{row} of $M$.
An OptiX instance transform is a $3\times4$ matrix stored row by row that acts
on column vectors, $p' = Tp$, with the translation in the last
\emph{column}. Since $pM = (M^{\mathsf T}p^{\mathsf T})^{\mathsf T}$, the
OptiX matrix is the transpose of USD's, without its last row (which, for an
affine transform, is $(0,0,0,1)$): $T_{rc} = M_{cr}$ for $r<3$ and $c<4$. The
matrix is computed in double precision and stored as floats.

\begin{sloppypar}Normals are not transformed here. They cannot be transformed with $M$ itself:
under a non-uniform scale, a vector perpendicular to the surface would stop
being perpendicular. The correct matrix is the inverse transpose,
$(M^{-1})^{\mathsf T}$, applied without translation. It keeps the transformed
normal perpendicular to every transformed tangent, because for a tangent $u$
and a normal $n$ with $u\cdot n=0$ we have $(uM)\cdot\bigl(n(M^{-1})^{\mathsf
T}\bigr)= u\,M M^{-1}\,n^{\mathsf T} = u\cdot n = 0$. The device programs
apply it at each hit, with OptiX's
[[optixTransformNormalFromObjectToWorldSpace]], which derives it from the
instance transform.\end{sloppypar}

<<Pass the transform of a mesh to the renderer>>=
// USD transforms row vectors (p * M); OptiX wants the transpose
const GfMatrix4d toWorld = xformCache.GetLocalToWorldTransform(p);
for (int r = 0; r < 3; ++r)
    for (int c = 0; c < 4; ++c)
        mesh.toWorld[4 * r + c] = float(toWorld[c][r]);
@ The camera needs to know where things are in the world: the bounds of the
mesh frame the selected prim, and the bounds of the scene frame everything.
The welded vertices are transformed in double precision, with
[[Transform]], which includes the translation, and the mesh's bounds grow to
include them; then the scene's bounds grow to include the mesh's.

<<Compute the world bounds of a mesh>>=
std::fill_n(mesh.boundsMin, 3, INFINITY);
std::fill_n(mesh.boundsMax, 3, -INFINITY);
for (size_t v = 0; v < mesh.vertexCount(); ++v)
{
    const GfVec3d w = toWorld.Transform(GfVec3d(mesh.positions[3 * v], mesh.positions[3 * v + 1],
                                                mesh.positions[3 * v + 2]));
    for (int k = 0; k < 3; ++k)
    {
        mesh.boundsMin[k] = std::min(mesh.boundsMin[k], float(w[k]));
        mesh.boundsMax[k] = std::max(mesh.boundsMax[k], float(w[k]));
    }
}
for (int k = 0; k < 3; ++k)
{
    lo[k] = std::min(lo[k], mesh.boundsMin[k]);
    hi[k] = std::max(hi[k], mesh.boundsMax[k]);
}
@ A prim whose material reads textures but which has no texture coordinates
would show one texel stretched over the whole surface. Better to say so.

<<Warn about textured prims without texture coordinates>>=
bool textured = false;
for (unsigned m : faceMaterial)
    textured = textured || m_materials[m].usesTextures();
if (textured && uvs.empty())
    warn("Textured prim without '" + uvSet + "' texture coordinates: " + p.GetPath().GetString());
@ The mesh receives the next mesh id, its index in [[m_meshes]], and the
index from paths learns it before the mesh is moved into the vector.

<<Register the mesh of a prim>>=
m_meshByPath[mesh.path] = unsigned(m_meshes.size());
m_meshes.push_back(std::move(mesh));
@ When the loop is over, the bounds are stored, but only if some geometry was
found; an empty scene keeps the zero box rather than an inverted infinite one.

<<Record the bounds of the scene>>=
if (!m_meshes.empty())
    for (int k = 0; k < 3; ++k)
    {
        m_boundsMin[k] = lo[k];
        m_boundsMax[k] = hi[k];
    }
@* Cameras. The scene keeps a list of [[SceneCamera]] records, one per
[[UsdGeomCamera]] prim. Like the geometry, each is evaluated once, at the
scene's time, and unlike the geometry it is stored in world space, because the
renderer reads it directly. A USD camera is described by its transform and by
physical lens parameters: a focal length and the size of the film back (the
\emph{aperture}), in tenths of a scene unit for orthographic cameras. What the
renderer wants is simpler: an eye point, a forward and an up direction, and
the half size of the \emph{window}, the part of the image plane that the
camera sees. For a perspective camera the window lies at distance 1, so its
half height is the tangent of half the vertical field of view; for an
orthographic camera it is measured in scene units. The viewport rarely has the
shape of the camera's film back, so the window is fitted to it later, in the
application. The path identifies the prim, and the vertical field of view is
kept for the inspector.

<<A camera of the stage>>=
// A UsdGeomCamera at the stage's start time, in world space. The window is
// the image plane at distance 1 (perspective) or the view size in scene
// units (orthographic); the viewer fits it to the viewport's aspect ratio.
struct SceneCamera
{
    std::string path;
    std::array<float, 3> eye = {0, 0, 0}, forward = {0, 0, -1}, up = {0, 1, 0};
    float halfWidth = 1.0f, halfHeight = 1.0f;
    bool orthographic = false;
    float clipNear = 0.0f, clipFar = 1e16f;
    float fovYDeg = 0.0f;  // vertical field of view of a perspective camera
};
@ USD already knows how to turn a camera prim into the quantities we need.
The method [[GetCamera]] of the [[UsdGeomCamera]] schema evaluates the prim's
attributes and its full transform into a [[GfCamera]], and the camera's
[[GfFrustum]] gives the eye position, the view and up directions, the window
and the clipping range. The frustum also discards any scale in the transform,
which USD requires: a camera under a scaled parent must still see with its own
lens. An aperture offset moves the window off centre (a shifted lens); the
renderer has no use for it, so it is reported rather than silently dropped.

The cameras are collected by a walk over the stage with the same
[[UsdPrimRange]] as the geometry. The range visits the prims depth first, in
the order of the file, so the camera menu lists the cameras as the file does.

<<Collect the cameras of the stage>>=
// ---- cameras ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Walks the hierarchy depth first, so cameras keep the order of the stage.
void Scene::extractCameras()
{
    for (const UsdPrim& p : UsdPrimRange::Stage(m_stage, UsdTraverseInstanceProxies()))
    {
        if (!p.IsA<UsdGeomCamera>())
            continue;
        // GfFrustum drops any scale in the camera's transform, as USD requires
        const GfCamera camera = UsdGeomCamera(p).GetCamera(m_time);
        const GfFrustum frustum = camera.GetFrustum();
        const GfVec3d eye = frustum.GetPosition(), forward = frustum.ComputeViewDirection(),
                      up = frustum.ComputeUpVector();
        SceneCamera c;
        c.path = p.GetPath().GetString();
        for (int k = 0; k < 3; ++k)
        {
            c.eye[k] = float(eye[k]);
            c.forward[k] = float(forward[k]);
            c.up[k] = float(up[k]);
        }
        <<Read the window and the clipping range of a camera>>
        m_cameras.push_back(c);
    }
}
@ [[GfCamera::GetFrustum]] already divides the aperture by the focal length
for a perspective camera, and converts it to scene units for an orthographic
one, so the window can be used as it is.

<<Read the window and the clipping range of a camera>>=
const GfRange2d window = frustum.GetWindow();
c.halfWidth = float(0.5 * window.GetSize()[0]);
c.halfHeight = float(0.5 * window.GetSize()[1]);
c.orthographic = frustum.GetProjectionType() == GfFrustum::Orthographic;
c.clipNear = float(frustum.GetNearFar().GetMin());
c.clipFar = float(frustum.GetNearFar().GetMax());
if (!c.orthographic)
    c.fovYDeg = camera.GetFieldOfView(GfCamera::FOVVertical);
if (window.GetMidpoint() != GfVec2d(0.0))
    warn("Camera aperture offsets are ignored: " + c.path);
